## Errors & Mistakes

The first selector test checked a supplied predecessor row but did not construct and walk the full public inference lattice. The final test covers the public recurrence.

The objective was initially described as sampled even though its negative construction is deterministic. The final description calls it candidate-augmented, teacher-forced cross-entropy.

Artifact loading was initially considered a possible validation signal. Loading alone did not establish functional proposal behavior, so the final exercise covered training, export, and serving. A small exact-recipe artifact had weak acceptance, which was treated as a data and optimization result rather than evidence of serving correctness.

Several configuration combinations initially reached training or export before failing. The final code rejects unsupported target widths, model types, layer layouts, token IDs, vocabulary changes, scales, and softcaps at the configuration boundary.

## Dead Ends

The public sources do not publish selector labels, a loss, negative construction, initialization, optimizer settings, or a training schedule. They cannot establish parity with a private training recipe.

Checkpoint loading and schema validation were insufficient functional gates. A bounded target-generated-data overfit diagnostic was useful for separating integration correctness from generalization, but it was not exact-recipe validation and does not establish production quality.

## Hints for Future Agents

Calling the selector without `training_successor_ids` must preserve the strict public top-K candidate set. Supplying `training_successor_ids` is a TorchSpec-only training augmentation that inserts the gold successor only when it is absent.

Do not claim parity with an unpublished upstream training recipe. Keep packed convolutions isolated at block boundaries, dispatch `DFlash2Config` before `DFlashConfig`, and preserve the public codebook keys without `.weight`.

For vLLM, an artifact with `block_size=B` requires `num_speculative_tokens=B-1` because the vLLM setting excludes the anchor.

## Codebase Discoveries

The public selector score combines a unary candidate score with a predecessor-conditioned bilinear term. The first predecessor is the verified anchor, and later predecessors are selected candidates from the previous position.

The public checkpoint omits the target embedding and language-model head and uses bare selector codebook names. Strict conversion must preserve that schema.

The supported SGLang path does not consume a non-unit DFlash2 input embedding scale, while the reviewed vLLM path does. Export therefore rejects unsupported SGLang configurations instead of silently changing behavior.

